Post-Hospital Deconditioning
Geriatric & Frailty
Profound new-onset weakness post-discharge, Decline in ADL independence, New requirement for mobility aid, Severe fatigue/reduced exercise tolerance

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-036
Reason for Visit: Acute functional collapse, inability to safely mobilize at home, and near-miss falls within 48 hours of hospital discharge.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient who was recently discharged from an acute hospital stay and is now demonstrating severe, unexpected mobility failure, rendering them unable to safely access the bathroom or manage basic needs. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out an acute medical complication of the discharge (e.g., hospital-acquired infection, acute DVT, or medication error) and prevent imminent traumatic falls or readmission prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I came home to recover, but I am so weak I can't even stand up to make a cup of tea, and my legs are like jelly."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports profound, generalized, and new-onset muscle weakness following a 14-day acute hospital admission for pneumonia. Prior to admission, they were independent; however, they now report a significant decline in the ability to perform basic activities of daily living independently. The adult child notes the patient has a severely slowed gait speed and a new requirement for a mobility aid (a frame) which they struggle to use correctly. The patient complains of severe fatigue and a dramatically reduced exercise tolerance, becoming breathless merely moving from the bed to the chair. They describe a noticeable loss of skeletal muscle mass (sarcopenia) particularly in the lower extremities. They experienced severe dizziness upon standing earlier today, consistent with orthostatic hypotension due to prolonged bed rest. Mild cognitive impairment (slowness in answering) is noted, likely residual from their acute illness. They also report a persistently poor appetite and weight loss over the admission.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; currently sitting in a chair and refusing to attempt transfers due to fear of falling.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls; inadequate immediate home support for this level of physical dependency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 (Supine) -> 95/60 (Standing) | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Appears frail, exhausted, and pale; movements are slow and deliberate.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals profound, symmetrical generalized muscle wasting (sarcopenia), predominantly affecting the quadriceps and gluteal muscles. Grip strength is markedly reduced bilaterally. The patient exhibits an unsteady, wide-based gait when assisted to stand, heavily relying on the walking frame. Cardiorespiratory exam reveals clear lungs (pneumonia resolved) and normal heart sounds. Neurological exam reveals no focal motor deficits, normal cranial nerves, and intact sensation. Orthostatic vitals confirm a 15mmHg systolic drop upon standing with symptomatic dizziness. (Comprehensive geriatric functional assessment demonstrating an objective drop in baseline mobility is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding).
Environmental Observations: The home has stairs to access the bedroom and a shower over the bath, making it currently unsafe for the patient's functional level.
Medication Reconciliation: Discharged on a new course of oral antibiotics and a recently up-titrated antihypertensive, contributing to the orthostatic drop.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Post-Hospital Deconditioning Syndrome - Status: Acute Exacerbation (Functional Decompensation)
Differentials:

Recurrent/Unresolved Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient is afebrile, lungs are clear on auscultation, SpO2 is acceptable on room air, and there is no purulent sputum production.

Acute Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical weakness without unilateral calf swelling, erythema, warmth, or localized tenderness.

Acute Stroke (CVA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical nature of the profound weakness and absolute lack of upper motor neuron signs or cranial nerve deficits.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Unsafe environment for current functional capacity)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic post-hospital deconditioning: profound generalized weakness, loss of ADL independence, and severe fatigue directly resulting from prolonged bed rest during an acute admission. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of a catastrophic fall due to this rapid functional decline. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in an unsafe environment with unmanaged orthostatic hypotension, highly likely resulting in a fall and a traumatic readmission. This urgent after-hours assessment allowed for the immediate adjustment of blood pressure medications, confirmed the absence of acute infectious/thromboembolic complications, and established safe, interim manual handling strategies for the family to manage the patient at home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Temporarily withheld the recently up-titrated antihypertensive medication to resolve the orthostatic hypotension and improve cerebral perfusion upon standing. Advised the family on safe, assisted transfer techniques (e.g., using a transfer belt). Ordered basic follow-up bloods (FBC, CMP) via the regular GP to ensure metabolic stability post-discharge.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured "sit, wait, stand" mobility protocol. Step 1: Before attempting to stand, the patient must sit on the edge of the bed/chair and pump their ankles vigorously ten times to promote venous return. Step 2: Upon successful completion of the ankle pumps, the patient stands with the frame, and the adult child provides immediate positive reinforcement by engaging in a short, preferred conversation topic. Cause-and-effect: Inserting a mandatory, physical counter-maneuver into the rising routine directly mitigates the orthostatic blood pressure drop, while the immediate social reward reinforces the deliberate slowing down of the transfer, drastically reducing the risk of a syncopal fall.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the Transition Care Program (TCP) or community physiotherapy for aggressive functional rehabilitation.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently recommended a shower chair and the temporary relocation of the bed to the ground floor.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48-72 hours to assess the medication adjustment and coordinate allied health rehabilitation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient experiences a sudden loss of consciousness, a fall resulting in head trauma or inability to bear weight, sudden chest pain, or a return of fever/productive cough.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your profound weakness is a direct physical result of being immobile in the hospital; your muscles have lost their conditioning, but there is no new acute illness today.
Clinical Course: Recovery from post-hospital deconditioning is a slow process that requires dedicated, daily physical rehabilitation. The dizziness should improve over the next 48 hours as we have stopped the extra blood pressure pill.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you completely pass out, hit your head in a fall, or develop a new high fever. You must see your regular GP next week to organize a physiotherapist to come to the house.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: When you are sick in a hospital bed, your muscles rapidly lose their strength and your blood vessels forget how to squeeze properly when you stand up. This causes the severe weakness and dizziness you are experiencing. We have stopped the blood pressure pill to help your circulation, but you must rebuild your muscle strength slowly.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to stand up quickly. Sit on the edge of the chair and pump your ankles ten times before rising. Your family must assist you when walking until the physiotherapist assesses you.

